<%# What the server tells the rest timer, and nothing else. #281.

    This element holds no state and draws nothing. It exists because the timer cannot live
    inside #session-body: a countdown is state, that region is replaced on every Done tap
    and every fifteen-second poll, and state inside a swapped element is state that resets
    -- the same reasoning that put the RPE footer outside it. So the timer sits outside and
    this carries the one fact it cannot work out for itself, which is that a set was just
    finished and what this lifter usually takes after one of that movement.

    Swapping it is therefore always safe. htmx replaces this span on every tap while the
    countdown above it runs untouched in a closure, which is exactly the split wanted: the
    server owns the suggestion, the browser owns the clock.

    data-set and data-at together are what make a cue new. The poll fires every fifteen
    seconds and swaps panels without touching this span, so the script cannot use "something
    was swapped" as its signal -- it would re-offer a timer for a set finished four minutes
    ago, on every poll, forever. Comparing the pair answers it exactly: a tap that finished a
    set carries a pair the script has not seen, and nothing else does.

    The pair rather than the stamp alone, because a stamp is not identity. Two sets finished
    inside the same second carry the same whole second, and a timer keyed on that would
    ignore the second tap and go on counting the first set's rest -- which is precisely what
    a superset tapped off in one motion looks like. The stamp is written to sub-second
    precision for the matching reason: a set un-completed and re-completed straight away is
    the same set with a new stamp, and that is a new rest.

    Empty on an un-complete, which is the case worth being explicit about. Tapping Done a
    second time takes a mis-tap back; that is not the end of a set and must not offer a
    rest. With no attributes the script finds no new stamp and does nothing -- and it
    deliberately does not stop a timer already running, because a mis-tap corrected is not a
    reason to lose the rest you are in the middle of.

    data-suggested is the number the timer defaults to and data-kind says where it came from.
    The programme's own rest wins; the median of this lifter's turnarounds is the fallback for
    a lift prescribing none. The kind has to travel with the number because the button says
    which it is -- "5:00 prescribed" and "3:10 your usual" are different claims, and showing
    one under the other's name would be the app passing its own measurement off as the
    programme's instruction, or the reverse.

    Both absent rather than zero where there is neither. A hand-logged session of a movement
    never trained before has no prescription and no history, and a zero would be read as a
    suggestion of none. The timer hides the suggestion button in that case and offers the
    plain durations, which is the honest thing to show somebody the app has not watched yet. %>
<%# Written as always-present attributes rather than conditional ones so that
    every value goes through ERB's escaping. A movement is named by the lifter -- exercises
    are user-created here -- so its name is untrusted text, and building the attribute as a
    raw string would be the one place on this screen where that was not true. Empty is the
    absent case: the script parses these and finds NaN, which is what it already tests for. %>
<%# `oob` is a local with a default so the two callers read the same way _progress does: the
    session view renders this plainly on first paint, and the complete route renders it with
    oob: true. It matters that the first paint does not carry the attribute -- htmx only
    performs out-of-band swaps on response content, so it would be inert either way, but an
    element claiming to be a swap target while sitting in the document it is a target in is
    a thing a reader has to rule out. %>
<span id="rest-cue"<%== ' hx-swap-oob="true"' if defined?(oob) && oob %> hidden
      data-set="<%= set_id %>" data-at="<%= at %>"
      data-suggested="<%= seconds %>" data-kind="<%= kind %>"
      data-movement="<%= movement %>"></span>
